[#:seon.task{:name "documentation-and-source-take-a-path-string"
             :plan "`(doc \"docs/x.md#anchor\")` and `(source \"docs/x.md#anchor\")` should answer a Markdown section at its doc and code levels, the same values `seon.ref/references` gives a one-backtick and a three-backtick citation (the lead, opening and headings below that `seon.ref/section` carries). Today `seon.sci.lookup/documentation-value` and `my.program/source` take a symbol or keyword only, so the reference levels' rule (a reference shows what the same typing at the REPL returns) does not hold for documents. Done when both take a path string with an optional anchor and a deftest asserts each equals the reference entry's lead, opening, below and text. Read `docs/seon/schedule/cards/TASK-CHAIN.md#3a-agent-verbs-and-what-references-show`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-section-citation-nests-references-of-the-text-it-shows"
             :plan "`seon.ref/resolve-path` still computes a section citation's nested `:seon.ref/references` through `seon.ref/inner` from the whole section text, although the doc level shows only the lead (or opening) and the headings below, so the entry names things it does not show. Done when the nested references come from the text the level shows (lead or opening at doc, the whole section at code) and a deftest cites a section whose body, not lead, names a function and asserts the doc entry does not carry it. `seon.ref-test/a-referents-own-references-show-one-level-for-every-text-kind` cites `AGENTS.md#data-and-contracts` today and must first move to a section with a lead."}]
